\section{Residualizing Recursive Calls Through a Reserved Entry}

Generalizing arguments controls how many specialization requests are possible, but it does not by itself make recursive requests terminate. A residual function can call itself with exactly the same specialization key while its body is being generated. Reserve its residual symbol before traversing the body rather than recursively waiting for the first request to finish.

\textbf{Inputs and assumptions.} A key combines a source function and its static arguments. A separate binding-time policy bounds the number of reachable keys and ensures that specialization of a body terminates aside from requests for other residual procedures. Residual signatures are known before bodies are emitted, and arguments marked dynamic remain parameters of those signatures. Keep all reserved symbols private until the complete unit passes validation.

\begin{sudocode}[Reserve before specializing a body]
\Require Demand for key $k$, memo table $M$
\Ensure Return a residual symbol, emitting at most one body
\If{$k$ is in $M$}
  \State \Return the symbol in $M[k]$ \Comment{Pending is enough to emit a call}
\EndIf
\Let{s}{\Call{NewSymbol}{\mathrm{signature}(k)}}
\State Store $s$ in $M$ as pending
\State Specialize the body, routing calls through this procedure
\If{specialization fails}
  \State Abort the private unit \Comment{No dangling calls are published}
\EndIf
\State Install the residual body for $s$ and mark it complete
\State \Return $s$
\end{sudocode}

\textbf{Example.} Specialize $f(k,n)$ for static $k=3$ and dynamic $n$, where $f(k,0)=0$ and $f(k,n)=k+f(k,n-1)$ for $n>0$. Reserve $f_3$ before descending into its two residual branches. The second branch becomes $3+f_3(n-1)$ using the pending reservation. Only one definition is emitted. Running it on $n=4$ returns 12.

\textbf{Invariant.} All residual references for a key point to the same symbol. The private unit is published only when every reservation has a body. This also supports mutual recursion: while emitting $f_3$, a request can reserve $g_3$, and the body of $g_3$ can call the still-pending $f_3$. Reserving entries handles repeated keys, not recursion that generates endlessly new static values; the separate key-bounding policy is essential.

The pending entry serves two roles in \Cref{fig:visual-96}: it prevents
another body from being generated and provides a symbol for the
recursive call.

