\section{Summary}\label{sec:demand-summary}

Demand-driven compilation shifts the compilation process from a batch operation to a lazy, on-demand service. By compiling only what is needed, when it is needed, and specializing based on the context of use, demand-driven compilers can reduce compilation time, improve code quality, and enable new programming paradigms like multi-stage programming. The theoretical foundations---partial evaluation, binding-time analysis, and staging type systems---provide a rigorous basis for these techniques.

\begin{historical}
Partial evaluation was studied extensively in the 1970s and 1980s, with landmark work by Futamura (1971), Jones, Gomard, and Sestoft (1993). Multi-stage programming was developed by Taha and Sheard (2000) in the MetaML language, and later refined in MetaOCaml. Lightweight modular staging (Rompf and Odersky, 2010) brought MSP to a wider audience by embedding it in Scala. The connection between partial evaluation and JIT compilation was realized by the Truffle framework, which uses partial evaluation to automatically transform interpreters into compilers. The trend is toward making staging more accessible, either through automatic partial evaluation (Truffle) or lightweight embedding (LMS), so that programmers can benefit from multi-stage computation without the complexity of explicit staging annotations.
\end{historical}
